// kyoten::frame::PadLock — the game letting go of a player's pad lock (the first slice's step 8, its sixth part, KYR-54;
// design/input.md § Decided, 2026-11-26, the sixth part's question 3). It is Output's own state, `"output": true` in the
// frame tier's records, as `TextEntry` and `Cursor` are: a "press A to continue" screen, two pads on the desk, the player
// picking up the other one. A game's prefab lists it on whatever entity asks: `"PadLock": { "player": 1 }`.
//
// The platform reads every one after each frame, any one asking letting go. The frame `letGo` turns on, the player's
// lock lets go, what the pad held is released, and the next pad pressed takes the player, as after a disconnect. Staying
// on does nothing more, so `lock.letGo = onTitleScreen` lets go each time the screen opens, and left on by mistake it
// changes nothing.

export { PadLock };

@generate(Serializable, Deserializable)
type value PadLock {
    // The player it is for, numbered from 1; 0 names none, and asks nothing.
    @field public int32 player = 0;
    // Whether the game lets go of that player's pad: each time it turns on, the lock lets go.
    @field public bool letGo = false;

    // Naming no player, and holding on.
    public default ctor make() { }
}
